{"id":"whole_vs_reduced_fat_milk","detail":"Whole milk is the one that had the least done to it. For years the guidance was ‘always low-fat dairy’, and the evidence behind that for otherwise-healthy people has weakened. Dairy fat is an area of honest debate, so whole milk is a whole-food-standard pick rather than a finding. What is not debated is the handling: skimming, standardizing and often ultra-pasteurizing are steps, and whole minimally-processed milk has fewer of them. A doctor’s advice for a specific person outranks a shelf standard. The old blanket advice to always choose low-fat dairy has softened, and the research on dairy fat is genuinely mixed.","science":"Reduced-fat, low-fat and skim are all whole milk with milkfat removed. They come from skimming cream off before homogenization and adding less back, never from adding water. The fat carries the flavor and most of the fullness. Dairy fat research is mixed, so the call rests on the processing, the checkable part. Grass-fed and pasture milk is a further layer, separate from the fat.","sources":["reviews of dairy-fat and cardiometabolic outcomes (contested literature)","Michigan State University Extension, dairy processing article (milk standardized to skim, 1%, 2% and whole through separators)","Florida Dairy Farmers “How Much Fat is in Whole Milk?” https://www.floridamilk.com/in-the-news/blog/nutrition/how-much-fat-is-in-whole-milk-whole-2-low-fat-and-skim-milk-explained.stml","characterization of the A1 and A2 beta-casein variants and BCM-7 release in digestion","human digestive-comfort trials on A2 milk (small, inconsistent, several industry-funded)","University of Minnesota Extension “A2 milk and A2 genetics” https://extension.umn.edu/agriculture/animals-and-livestock/dairy/a2-milk-and-a2-genetics","FDA Pasteurized Milk Ordinance (time and temperature standards)","FDA milk labeling and processing definitions","Michigan State University Extension, dairy processing article https://www.canr.msu.edu/news/they_did_what_to_your_milk (homogenization forces milk at high pressure through small holes so fat does not rise; no health benefits or risks associated with it; non-homogenized milk available from some bottlers)","CDC “Raw Milk” https://www.cdc.gov/food-safety/foods/raw-milk.html"]}
{"id":"dairy_case","detail":"Under 21 CFR 131.110, adding vitamins A and D to milk is optional. If vitamin D is added, each quart holds 400 International Units (IU), a measure of vitamin amount. Under 7 CFR 205.240, organic dairy animals must graze pasture throughout the grazing season. That season must be at least 120 days per year. Ultrafiltered milk is pushed through a porous membrane under pressure, a process called ultrafiltration. It changes the water, lactose and some minerals, and concentrates the protein already in the milk. The label then shows more protein, less sugar and a longer shelf life.","science":"Adding vitamins A and D to milk is optional, so the label tells. If vitamin D is added, each quart holds 400 International Units. Organic dairy animals must graze pasture throughout a grazing season of at least 120 days per year. Ultrafiltration pushes milk through a porous membrane, concentrating the protein already in it. The label then shows more protein, less sugar and a longer shelf life.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/131.110","https://www.dairyreporter.com/Article/2026/03/13/how-ultrafiltered-milk-is-made-benefits-protein-process/","https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/7/205.240","https://www.farmanddairy.com/?p=14321"]}
{"id":"cheese_real_vs_processed","detail":"Read the name and the list. Two different additions sit behind the same aisle. ‘Process cheese’ is an emulsified blend and is a different product from cheese. Pre-shredded is genuine cheese with a coating applied so the strands do not knit back together in the bag, and that coating is exactly why bagged shreds never melt as smoothly as cheese grated at home. Neither is a safety question. Both are a reason to buy the block and spend thirty seconds with a grater. ‘Parmesan’ is a generic word in the US; ‘Parmigiano-Reggiano’ is a protected one with a stamped rind. The shred coating is usually potato starch or powdered cellulose, plus natamycin as a mold inhibitor. Both are edible. The block is cheaper per pound, melts properly, and is the one to buy.","science":"Real cheese is milk, salt, cultures and enzymes. ‘Pasteurized process cheese product’ needs only 51% cheese, and the name on the front is the only warning. Shreds carry a coating so the strands do not knit back together, which is why they resist melting. ‘Parmesan’ is a generic word in the US; ‘Parmigiano-Reggiano’ is protected, with a stamped rind.","sources":["FDA standards of identity for cheese","FDA process-cheese labeling rules","Mason Dixie Foods “Natural Cheese vs. Processed” https://masondixiefoods.com/blogs/faqs/natural-cheese-vs-processed"]}
{"id":"yogurt_plain_vs_flavored","detail":"Plain yogurt, especially Greek or strained, is a whole food: milk and cultures. The added sugar in fruit-on-the-bottom and flavored cups is where it goes sideways, and the gap between the plain tub and the fruit cup on the added-sugars line is the entire decision. Whether the cultures in that tub are still alive is a separate question, read from the ingredient list and the seal.","science":"Plain still lists sugar, and it is lactose, the milk’s own. On flavored, it is lactose plus what was added, so only the added-sugars line separates the two. ‘Light’ means a high-intensity sweetener like sucralose, not a plainer cup. Fruit-on-the-bottom and drinkable yogurts can run higher in sugar than the pre-mixed cup.","sources":["USDA FoodData Central","FDA added-sugars labeling requirements","University of Florida IFAS Extension “All About Yogurt” (FS185) https://ask.ifas.ufl.edu/publication/FS185","FDA standards of identity for yogurt","National Yogurt Association ‘Live & Active Cultures’ seal criteria"]}
{"id":"ice_cream","detail":"Under 21 CFR 135.110, ice cream holds at least 10 percent milkfat and at least 4.5 pounds to the gallon. The pounds-per-gallon floor limits how much air is whipped in. Ice cream excludes other food fats, except incidental amounts. Frozen custard is ice cream with 1.4 percent egg yolk solids. A frozen dairy dessert may list oils such as coconut, palm or soybean oil.","science":"The name “ice cream” requires at least 10 percent milkfat and 4.5 pounds to the gallon. The pounds-per-gallon floor limits how much air is whipped in. Ice cream excludes other food fats, except incidental amounts. A product short of the standard is sold as “frozen dairy dessert”, with more air and less milk fat. It may list oils such as coconut, palm or soybean oil.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/135.110","https://www.mentalfloss.com/food/ice-cream/ice-cream-vs-frozen-dairy-dessert-what-is-difference"]}
{"id":"cream_vs_creamer","detail":"Heavy cream is at least 36% milkfat, light whipping 30 to 36, half-and-half 10.5 to 18. Standards of identity fix what cream can mean, which is why those fat percentages are consistent across every carton. Creamer has no such standard because it is a formulated product. The oil in it is usually a refined seed oil, and the sweetness usually comes from corn syrup solids or sugar, with the flavored versions adding more of both. Some cartons of real cream carry stabilizers like carrageenan or gellan gum, so a short list is worth checking on the dairy side too. Whole milk, half-and-half or real cream all do the job with a list you can read out loud. Read the two ingredient lists side by side and it takes three seconds.","science":"Cream is dairy with a legal fat minimum fixed by standards of identity. ‘Non-dairy creamer’ has no such standard because it is a formulated product. Water, sugar and vegetable oil are the usual first three ingredients, with emulsifiers and thickeners. Many are built on almond, oat or coconut milk rather than cream. Whole milk or half-and-half does the same job with a two-word list.","sources":["FDA standards of identity for cream products, 21 CFR 131","FDA food labeling regulations","Product ingredient labeling","Morning Fresh Dairy “Coffee Creamer, Half & Half, and Whipping Cream” https://www.morningfreshdairy.com/moo-news/an-explanation-on-coffee-creamer-half-half-and-whipping-cream.html"]}
{"id":"cottage_cheese","detail":"Under 21 CFR 133.128, cottage cheese is dry curd mixed with a creaming mixture. It holds at least 4 percent milkfat and at most 80 percent moisture. The label states “not less than __ percent milkfat” with the name. Under 21 CFR 133.129, the curd is set by lactic acid bacteria or by food-grade acids. Direct acidification means an acid, not bacteria, sets the curd. That method is labeled “Directly set” or “Curd set by direct acidification”. Dry curd cottage cheese has less than 0.5 percent milkfat.","science":"Cottage cheese is dry curd mixed with a creaming mixture. It holds at least 4 percent milkfat and at most 80 percent moisture. The curd is set by lactic acid bacteria or by food-grade acids. An acid-set curd is labeled “Directly set” or “Curd set by direct acidification”. Without those words, a culture set it. Dry curd has no creaming mixture.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/133.128","https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/133.129"]}
{"id":"sour_cream","detail":"Under 21 CFR 131.160, sour cream is pasteurized cream soured by lactic acid bacteria. It may be named “Cultured sour cream”. Under 21 CFR 131.162, acidified sour cream is soured with acidifiers, with or without lactic acid bacteria. An acidifier is an added food-grade acid. Both hold at least 18 percent milkfat. Optional ingredients may improve texture, extend shelf life, or stop syneresis, which is liquid separating out. A flavoring that characterizes the product is declared with the name.","science":"Federal standards name two ways to sour cream. Sour cream, or cultured sour cream, is pasteurized cream soured by lactic acid bacteria. Acidified sour cream is soured with added food-grade acids, with or without bacteria. Both hold at least 18 percent milkfat. Optional ingredients may improve texture, extend shelf life, or stop liquid separating out. Culturing is the traditional way to sour cream.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/131.160","https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/131.162"]}
{"id":"cream_cheese","detail":"Under 21 CFR 133.133, cream cheese is a soft, uncured cheese. It holds at least 33 percent milkfat and at most 55 percent moisture. Its dairy ingredients are pasteurized and set with a lactic acid bacterial culture. Stabilizers may total no more than 0.5 percent of the finished food. American Neufchâtel, sold in the United States, closely resembles cream cheese. French Neufchâtel ripens and grows a soft rind.","science":"Cream cheese is a soft, fresh cheese that does not mature. Its standard requires at least 33 percent milkfat and at most 55 percent moisture. The dairy ingredients are pasteurized and set with a lactic acid bacterial culture. Stabilizers may total no more than 0.5 percent of the finished food. American Neufchâtel closely resembles cream cheese; French Neufchâtel ripens and grows a soft rind.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/133.133","https://www.masterclass.com/articles/neufchatel-cheese-vs-cream-cheese-explained"]}
{"id":"almond_milk","detail":"Almond milk holds about 0.4 grams of protein per 100 ml. It is usually fortified with added vitamins and minerals. FDA notes that almond- and oat-based drinks are not part of the dairy group in the Dietary Guidelines. Their overall nutritional content is not similar to milk. FDA suggests choosing drinks higher in protein, vitamin D, calcium and potassium. It also suggests lower saturated fat and added sugars. FDA notes the name “almond milk” is established by common usage.","science":"Almond milk is watery, with a nutty flavor and no lactose. It holds about 0.4 grams of protein per 100 ml. It is usually fortified with added vitamins and minerals. FDA does not place almond drinks in the dairy group, and their overall nutrition is not similar to milk. FDA suggests drinks higher in protein, vitamin D, calcium and potassium, and lower in saturated fat and added sugars.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Almond_milk","https://www.fda.gov/food/food-labeling-nutrition/plant-based-milk-alternatives-pbma"]}
{"id":"oat_milk","detail":"The paper describes production that can include milling, enzyme hydrolysis, formulation, and heat treatment. It notes that oat drinks generally contain less protein than cow’s milk and may have added vitamins and minerals. The study measured sensory, physical, and chemical properties, not health outcomes.","science":"Oat drinks vary in composition and fortification; the carton is the basis for choosing. Production can include milling, enzyme hydrolysis, formulation and heat treatment. Oat drinks generally contain less protein than cow’s milk and may have added vitamins and minerals. The sole paper studied six original UK products, excluding barista, flavored and sugar-free versions. It measured sensory, physical and chemical properties, not health outcomes, and sets no universal health ranking.","sources":["https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC10877596/fullTextXML"]}
{"id":"soy_milk","detail":"Soy milk is made by soaking and grinding soybeans, boiling the mixture and filtering out particles. Its protein content runs 1–4 percent, depending on the method of production. It may be sweetened, flavored and fortified with micronutrients. FDA notes the Dietary Guidelines include only fortified soy beverages in the dairy group. The reason given is that they have key nutrients similar to those in milk. FDA suggests choosing drinks higher in protein, vitamin D, calcium and potassium. It also suggests lower saturated fat and added sugars.","science":"Soy milk is made by soaking and grinding soybeans, boiling the mixture and filtering out particles. Its protein runs 1–4 percent, depending on the method of production. It may be sweetened, flavored and fortified with micronutrients. FDA notes the Dietary Guidelines count only fortified soy drinks in the dairy group. The reason given is key nutrients similar to those in milk.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soy_milk","https://www.fda.gov/food/food-labeling-nutrition/plant-based-milk-alternatives-pbma"]}
{"id":"coconut_milk_beverage","detail":"Canned coconut milk is made by steeping shredded coconut in water or milk, then pressing it. The carton coconut milk beverage blends coconut milk with water and added ingredients. Those can include sugar, flavors, vitamins, preservatives and thickeners such as gellan gum. The thickeners make it creamy despite the added water. It has about an eighth of the fat of canned coconut milk. Tasters found the unsweetened carton surprisingly bland. One test kitchen’s bottom line: do not cook with the coconut milk beverage.","science":"Canned coconut milk is shredded coconut steeped in water or milk, then pressed. The carton beverage blends coconut milk with water and added ingredients. Those can include sugar, flavors, vitamins, preservatives and thickeners such as gellan gum. The thickeners make it creamy despite the added water. It has about an eighth of the fat of the can. It does not stand in for canned coconut milk in cooking.","sources":["https://www.cookscountry.com/how_tos/6667-canned-coconut-milk-vs-carton-packed-coconut-milk-beverage","https://www.tastingtable.com/1527981/canned-coconut-milk-carton-difference"]}

#!/usr/bin/env node
// Generate server/lib/doLines.json from docs/do-lines-review.md.
//
//   node scripts/buildDoLines.js
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: THE DEPLOY BOUNDARY. Railway's Root Directory is `server/`, so
// nothing outside it reaches production. Both the counter's card projection and its ask
// pipeline read the reviewed `do` lines from `docs/`, wrapped in a try/catch that fell
// back to an empty Map — so in production every curated card served by /api/counter/ask
// had an EMPTY do line. The do line is the whole point of the card, and asking is the
// lead interaction on the Counter. It had been that way since the lines were authored.
//
// The markdown stays the AUTHORED SOURCE, because it is reviewed by hand and its table
// is the reviewable artifact. This JSON is a build product of it that lives inside the
// deploy boundary, and `doLines.test.js` fails if the two disagree — so the generated
// file cannot drift from the reviewed one.
//
// Run this after editing the review table, and commit the result.

import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';
import { dirname, join } from 'node:path';

import { parseReviewTable } from '../lib/counterCards.js';

const __dirname = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));
const REVIEW = join(__dirname, '..', '..', 'docs', 'do-lines-review.md');
const OUT = join(__dirname, '..', 'lib', 'doLines.json');

const reviewed = parseReviewTable(readFileSync(REVIEW, 'utf8'));
if (!reviewed.size) {
  console.error('[do-lines] the review table parsed to nothing — refusing to write an empty file.');
  process.exit(1);
}

// Slug -> do line. The `flag` column is review metadata and is deliberately not carried:
// production needs the line, not the notes about drafting it.
const out = {};
for (const slug of [...reviewed.keys()].sort()) {
  const line = String(reviewed.get(slug)?.do || '').trim();
  if (line) out[slug] = line;
}

writeFileSync(OUT, `${JSON.stringify(out, null, 2)}\n`, 'utf8');
console.log(`[do-lines] wrote ${Object.keys(out).length} do lines to lib/doLines.json ✓`);
// Pass rule for the readability rewrites (VOICE_SPEC "Readable in the aisle"): rephrasing, never deletion.
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import { CULINARY_LEXICON } from '../../lib/counterCardLint.js';
import kb from '../../kristy_perimeter_kb.json' with { type: 'json' };

export const FROZEN = ['id', 'sources', 'aliases', 'asked_as', 'category', 'evidence_tier', 'cart_pick', 'instead', 'labels_decoded'];
export const REWRITABLE = ['decision', 'short_answer', 'why', 'buying_tips', 'watch_out', 'detail', 'kristy_take', 'tier_note', 'eyebrow_short', 'question'];
// Health-first REWRITE ids (docs/plans/card-health-first-plan.md, Piece 4): the approved plan
// re-aims cart_pick, adds asked_as/sources, drops cook-verb aliases and re-words label meanings.
// 4a, 4b and 4c ids; listed together so C12 re-anchors once.
export const HEALTH_FIRST_REWRITE = new Set([
  'ground_beef_lean_ratio', 'beef_cuts_basics', 'beef_grades_usda', 'meat_case', 'butcher_counter_asking',
  'pick_soup_bones', 'egg_grades_sizes', 'pork_cuts_and_enhanced', 'lamb_goat',
  'flour_basics', 'cooking_oils', 'salt', 'sugars', 'maple_syrup', 'cocoa', 'tea', 'broth', 'grains_beyond_rice',
  'bagels', 'pretzels', 'kimchi', 'miso', 'pick_tofu', 'goat_sheep_dairy', 'coconut_milk_beverage',
  // chf-5 fold absorbers: aliases/asked_as gained from retired cards (additive; keepsAll guards losses).
  'chicken_cuts_basics', 'beans_dried_vs_canned',
]);
// Item-card fold survivors and ladder fronts (docs/ITEM-CARDS-PLAN.md IC-3..IC-7): they gain
// aliases/asked_as/sources/labels_decoded from folded cards. chicken_cuts_basics and
// beef_cuts_basics are already in HEALTH_FIRST_REWRITE.
export const ITEM_CARD_REWRITE = new Set([
  'produce_ripeness_by_item', 'berries_picking', 'egg_labels', 'judging_meat_at_the_case', 'seafood_counter',
  'whole_vs_reduced_fat_milk', 'yogurt_plain_vs_flavored', 'plain_kefir', 'organic_worth_it_by_type',
  'beef_grassfed_vs_grainfed', 'salmon_wild_vs_farmed', 'shrimp_imported_vs_domestic', 'grassfed_butter',
  'produce_apples_pears', 'produce_citrus', 'produce_peppers', 'produce_leafy_greens', 'produce_root_vegetables',
  'produce_brassicas', 'produce_onions_garlic', 'produce_green_beans', 'produce_mushrooms', 'produce_stone_fruit',
]);
// Card-alignment rewrites (docs/CARD-ALIGNMENT-PLAN.md milestone 1).
export const CARD_ALIGN_REWRITE = new Set([
  'beef_grassfed_vs_grainfed', 'ground_beef_lean_ratio', 'beef_cuts_basics', 'organ_meats', 'butcher_counter_asking',
  'judging_meat_at_the_case', 'bison', 'venison_game', 'lamb_goat', 'pick_soup_bones',
  'pork_cuts_and_enhanced', 'pick_sausage', 'pick_jerky', 'chicken_cuts_basics', 'chicken_breast',
  'rotisserie_chicken', 'turkey_whole', 'ground_turkey',
  'bacon', 'ham', 'hot_dogs', 'turkey_bacon', 'cured_pork', 'deli_meat_uncured',
  'egg_labels', 'egg_duck_quail', 'pick_egg_whites',
  'whole_vs_reduced_fat_milk', 'raw_milk', 'yogurt_plain_vs_flavored', 'grassfed_butter', 'ghee', 'plain_kefir',
  'raw_kefir', 'sour_cream', 'cream_cheese', 'cottage_cheese', 'cheese_real_vs_processed', 'raw_aged_cheese',
  'goat_sheep_dairy', 'cream_vs_creamer', 'ice_cream', 'dairy_case',
  'almond_milk', 'oat_milk', 'soy_milk', 'coconut_milk_beverage', 'plant_butter',
]);
// alias → ids carrying it in the current KB: an item-card id may drop an alias that moved to another card.
const ALIAS_OWNERS = new Map();
for (const e of kb.entries || []) {
  for (const a of e.aliases || []) {
    if (!ALIAS_OWNERS.has(a)) ALIAS_OWNERS.set(a, new Set());
    ALIAS_OWNERS.get(a).add(e.id);
  }
}
const RELAXED = ['cart_pick', 'asked_as', 'aliases', 'sources', 'labels_decoded'];
const CA_RELAXED = ['cart_pick', 'instead', 'asked_as', 'aliases', 'labels_decoded'];
// By value: sources may be {url, ...} objects, which includes() compares by reference.
const keepsAll = (newList, oldList) => {
  const have = new Set((newList || []).map((x) => JSON.stringify(x)));
  return (oldList || []).every((x) => have.has(JSON.stringify(x)));
};

const NUMBER = /\d+(?:[.,]\d+)?%?/g;
const isEmpty = (v) => v == null || (typeof v === 'string' ? v.trim() === '' : Array.isArray(v) && v.length === 0);

export function assertPassRule(t, oldEntry, newEntry) {
  const id = oldEntry.id;
  const hf = HEALTH_FIRST_REWRITE.has(id) || ITEM_CARD_REWRITE.has(id);
  const ca = CARD_ALIGN_REWRITE.has(id);
  const rewrite = hf || ca;
  for (const f of FROZEN) {
    if ((hf && RELAXED.includes(f)) || (ca && CA_RELAXED.includes(f))) continue;
    assert.deepEqual(newEntry[f], oldEntry[f], `${id}: frozen field ${f} changed`);
  }
  if (rewrite) {
    assert.ok(keepsAll(newEntry.asked_as, oldEntry.asked_as), `${id}: asked_as lost a phrasing`);
    assert.ok(keepsAll(newEntry.sources, oldEntry.sources), `${id}: sources lost an entry`);
    for (const a of oldEntry.aliases || []) {
      if (!(newEntry.aliases || []).includes(a)) {
        assert.ok(
          CULINARY_LEXICON.test(a) || (ITEM_CARD_REWRITE.has(id) && [...(ALIAS_OWNERS.get(a) ?? [])].some((o) => o !== id)),
          `${id}: alias "${a}" dropped but is not a cook-verb alias and no other card carries it`
        );
      }
    }
    const terms = (newEntry.labels_decoded || []).map((l) => l.term);
    for (const l of oldEntry.labels_decoded || []) assert.ok(terms.includes(l.term), `${id}: label term "${l.term}" dropped`);
  }
  for (const [f, v] of Object.entries(oldEntry)) {
    if (!isEmpty(v)) assert.ok(!isEmpty(newEntry[f]), `${id}: ${f} was non-empty and is now empty`);
  }
  assert.ok((newEntry.watch_out || []).length >= (oldEntry.watch_out || []).length, `${id}: watch_out lost an item`);
  const oldText = REWRITABLE.map((f) => JSON.stringify(oldEntry[f] ?? '')).join(' ');
  // Rewritable fields only: a number surviving in a source URL is not a surviving fact.
  const newText = REWRITABLE.map((f) => JSON.stringify(newEntry[f] ?? '')).join(' ');
  for (const n of new Set(oldText.match(NUMBER) || [])) {
    assert.ok(newText.includes(n), `${id}: number ${n} from the old text is gone`);
  }
}

